Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are currently receiving more and more attention as sensing materials for their highly ordered crystalline architectures, high porosity, abundant active sites, and adjustable structures and functions.23−28 Various homochiral MOFs have been made either by using chiral organic ligands as starting building blocks or by introducing chiral ingredients through post-synthetic modification of achiral MOFs. The chirality has allowed remarkable performances of MOFs in asymmetric catalysis, enantioselective sorption and separation, and other applications.29−33 However, the studies using chiral MOFs for electrochemical sensors have been rather limited. Only several chiral MOFs, assembled from pure chiral linkers or mixed chiral/achiral linkers or obtained by post-synthetic chiral modification, have been demonstrated to be effective electrode modifiers, allowing enantiomeric recognition of some chiral substrates (e.g., $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\overline{{(\mathrm{cam)}_{2}}}\mathrm{dabco}]_{n},$ $[\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{4}]_{n}$ $\mathrm{~\small~\leftmoon~}=\mathrm{~\small~N~}$ -(2-hydroxybenzyl)-L-leucine), urchin$\mathrm{CMOF/rGO}_{\cdot}$ , $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{L}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{G l u}/\mathsf{M I L}\mathsf{-}125\left(\mathrm{Ti}\right)/\mathrm{TiN}\mathsf{M},}$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{MIL}$ -125- NH-L-His, and $\big[\mathrm{Ag(s,r\mathrm{-}L)}\big](\mathrm{PF}_{6}){\cdot}3\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{O}_{2}{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ .34−39 However, the recognition selectivity is still low. It is a great scientific challenge to construct a chiral microenvironment for efficient enantioselective molecular recognition, and even more challenging is to find the way for efficient translation of the recognition to an electrochemical response.
L-Tryptophan $({\mathrm{Trp}})$ is one of the essential amino acids in humans .40−42 It is the precursor of serotonin, niacin, melatonin, etc., and has very important physiological and biochemical functions in the human body. D-Trp mainly exists in plants and is hardly metabolized in the human body, and its existence affects the formation of biofilm and the growth of bacteria.43,44 As a consequence, it is very important to devise a sensor that can effectively recognize tryptophan isomers.
In this work, we report the distinct electrochemical enantiomeric recognition performance of two isomorphic homochiral MOFs toward Trp. The MOFs, Cu-TBPBe and $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBa, have the same L-proline-derived enantiopure dicarboxylate linker terephthaloyl-bis(L-proline) $\left(\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{TB}\bar{P}r o}\right)$ but different bipyridyl linkers, 1,2-bis(4-bipyridyl)ethene (bpee) and 1,2-bis(4-bipyridyl)ethane (bpea), respectively. The electrode modified with Cu-TBPBe displays an unprecedented high selectivity for enantiomeric recognition $(I_{\mathrm{L-Trp}}/I_{\mathrm{p-Trp}}$ up to 53) and a very sensitive detection ability toward L-Trp. It can be a reliable chiral sensing interface for the sensitive quantification of L-Trp in the racemic mixtures. On the contrary, Cu-TBPBa is incapable of enantiomeric discrimination. It is rather unexpected that the subtle distinction in achiral ligands (ethene versus ethane tether between pyridyl donors) leads to so remarkable differences in chiral recognition performance. According to structural and spectral analyses, the flat achiral bpee ligand and the chiral proline moieties around it collaboratively set up a chiral microenvironment in favor of $\mathrm{~L~}$ -Trp adsorption through multiple $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and hydrogen-bonding interactions. The study highlights the great importance of achiral ingredients in determining the chiral recognition ability of chiral MOFs.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Structure. Single-crystal X-ray crystallographic analysis reveals that $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe and $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBa are isomorphic; only the structure of $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe is described in detail. $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe crystalizes in the orthorhombic system chiral space group $P2_{1}2_{1}2_{1}$ . There is one $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ ion, one $\mathrm{TBPro}^{2-}$ anion, one bpee, and one coordination water molecule in the asymmetric unit. The five-coordinated $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ ion is situated in the center of a square pyramid and surrounded by the two carboxyl oxygen atoms of two $\mathrm{TBPro}^{2-}$ anions and two nitrogen atoms of two bpee ligands in the basal plane of the pyramid, as well as the oxygen atom of a coordination water molecule at the apex of the pyramid. Taking into account the weak coordination related to the longer $\operatorname{Cul-O2}$ distance of $2.867\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ the Cu1 ion becomes a distorted octahedron (Figure S2). Each $\mathrm{TBPro}^{2-}$ anion links two $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ ions through two carboxylate groups in the bidentate and monodentate coordination modes to form a 1D left-handed helical chain $[\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{TBPro})]_{n}$ with a pitch of 17.509 Å along the $b$ -axis (Figure 1a). The bpee ligand also connects $\mathtt{C u}(\bar{\Pi})$ ions into a 1D left-handed helical chain $[\mathrm{Cu(bpee)}]^{2+}{_{n}}$ with a pitch of $26.696\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -axis (Figure 1b). The $\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{TBPro}{\bar{)}}\right]_{n}$ and $[\mathrm{Cu(bpee)}]^{2+}{}_{n}^{\circ}$ helical chains are interweaved together by sharing the $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ ions to build a 3D framework (Figure 1c,d). In $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBa, the bpea ligands link $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ ions to also form a 1D left-handed helical chain $\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{bpea})\right]^{2+}n$ with a pitch of $26.537\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -axis (Figure S3). The 3D framework presents a diamond topology, which is based on the consideration of $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ ions as 4-connecting nodes and the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{TBPro}^{2-}$ anions and bpee or bpea ligands as 2- connecting linkers (Figure S4).
Electrochemical Characterization of $\mathsf{c u}$ -TBPBe. The electrochemical performance of $\mathtt{C u}$ -TBPBe@NF/GCE was analyzed by cyclic voltammetry (CV) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) in $10~\mathrm{\mL}$ of $\textit{s}\mathrm{mM}$ [Fe$(\bar{\mathrm{CN}})_{6}]^{4-}/^{3-}$ solution containing $0.1\mathrm{~M~KCl}$ as a supporting electrolyte. As presented in Figure 2a, the CV curve of CuTBPBe $(\varpi\mathrm{NF}$ /GCE shows a pair of well-defined redox peaks that originated from the $\mathrm{Fe(\bar{C}N)_{6}^{~4-}/F e(C N)_{6}^{~3-}}$ couple. The peak potentials and currents are similar to those at the bare GCE, which indicates that the modification with $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe does not significantly affect the reversibility and activity of the redox couple. EIS analysis (Figure 2b) revealed that CuTBPBe@NF/GCE gives a charge transfer resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ value of $236.63\Omega$ , somewhat higher than the value of the bare GCE $\left(100.9~\Omega\right)$ . Nevertheless, the value is still relatively low, indicating that the modified interfaces can allow fast charge transfer kinetics.
Enantioselective Recognition of Trp Isomers with CuTBPBe. The CV curves of Trp enantiomers were measured in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ PBS $\mathrm{\Phi}_{\mathrm{pH}}=7.0\$ ). Trp shows a sluggish redox kinetics at the electrodes without Cu-TBPBe modification, as indicated by the very weak CV signals (Figure S5). However, CuTBPBe@NF/GCE produces obvious peaks, confirming a high electrocatalytic activity of the MOF for the redox of Trp. Both $\mathrm{~L~}$ -Trp and D-Trp show high reduction peaks and low oxidation peaks, suggesting the poor reversibility of the electrochemical reactions. To our delight, L-Trp gives significantly higher reduction peak current than D-Trp, with a current ratio $(I_{\mathrm{L-Trp}}/$ $I_{\mathrm{{D-Trp}}})$ of $2.9\pm0.2$ $\left(n=3\right)$ (Figure 3). This indicates that CuTBPBe ${\mathfrak{D N F}}/{\mathrm{GCE}}$ has a high efficiency for chiral recognition between the Trp enantiomers. Figure S6 shows the CVs of LTrp at Cu-TBPBe@NF/GCE for 30 scan cycles. The absence of any obvious change during the continuous scans proves the excellent electrochemical stability of the modified electrode.
The influence of $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ on electrochemical recognition was studied (Figure S7). First, PXRD patterns demonstrate the structural integrity of $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe in PBS at different $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ levels (Figure S8). For both L-Trp and D-Trp, the reduction peak potential is shifted negatively with an increase in $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ from 5.0 to 8.0, and the peak current intensity witnesses a maximum at the $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ of 7.0. At any $\mathrm{\tt{pH}},$ , $\mathrm{~L~}$ -Trp produces higher peak current than the D-isomer. The current ratio $(I_{\mathrm{L-Trp}}/\breve{I}_{\mathrm{D-Trp}})^{\bullet}$ reaches the maximum also at $\mathrm{pH}7.0\$ , which was selected as the optimum $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ for further experiments. The effect of the scan rate was checked to probe the redox process of L-Trp at Cu-TBPBe $@$ NF/GCE (Figure S9). The reduction peak current intensity $\left(I_{\mathrm{p}}\right)$ increases with scan rate, with a good linear relationship for $I_{\mathrm{p}}$ versus $\nu^{1/2}$ . This suggests a typical diffusion-controlled process.45−48
The quantitative determination by CV with Cu-TBPBe $@$ NF/GCE was examined. As illustrated in Figure 4 and Figure S10, the reduction peak current intensity of both $\mathrm{~L~}$ -Trp and DTrp increases with their concentration increasing from $10^{-7}$ to $10^{\Bar{-}3}\mathrm{~M},$ , and regression analyses displayed different linear correlations of current with the logarithm of concentration. The limit of detection (LOD) was estimated to be $0.067\ \mu\mathrm{M}$ for L-Trp and $0.25~\mu\mathrm{M}$ for D-Trp $\left(3\sigma/k\right)$ .27,49,50 The LOD for L-Trp is among the lowest reported for electrochemical sensors for Trp enantiomer s.51−54 The large LOD difference between
L-Trp and D-Trp reflects the chiral recognition ability of the sensor.
The chiral recognition can be significantly amplified by using the method of differential pulse voltammetry (DPV). As observed from Figure 5, $\mathtt{C u}$ -TBPBe@NF/GCE exhibits a very weak DPV response to D-Trp but a very strong current response to L-Trp, resulting in a very high $I_{\mathrm{L-Trp}}/I_{\mathrm{D-Trp}}$ ratio of $53\pm2$ $(n=3)$ ). The high recognition ratio was confirmed with five independently prepared $\mathtt{C u}$ -TBPBe@NF/GCE, with a relative standard deviation (RSD) of $4.0\%$ (Figure S11). This suggests a good reproducibility of the sensing interface. Such a high enantioselective recognition efficiency is rare in electrochemical sensing. Previous electrodes for chiral discrimination of Trp have been fabricated by modification with chiral peptides,21,55 organosilica,5 6 carbohydrates,57−59 conducting polymers,60−62 metal complexes,36,63,64 and a ZIF-type MOF doped with D-histidine.53 The peak current ratios for Trp isomers fall in the range of 2−14 (Table S5). The high value of
53 observed for Cu-TBPBe represents a big step forward in the pursuit of high-efficiency chiral electrochemical sensing.
The excellent enantioselectivity of the DPV method using Cu-TBPBe is confirmed at various Trp concentrations (Figure 6). The DPV response to L-Trp is always much stronger and increases much more rapidly with concentration than that to DTrp. The slopes of the linear peak current versus $\log(C)$ plots differ by nearly 42 times. Therefore, L-Trp can be sensitively detected, the estimated DPV-based LOD being as low as 3.16 nM. The detection is hardly affected by D-Trp, which gives a too weak DPV response to be precisely detected. These results confirm that the Cu-TBPBe@NF/GCE sensing interface possesses a very high electrochemical recognition efficiency and sensitive sensing ability toward Trp enantiomers.
The chiral recognition performances of the Cu-TBPBe $@$ NF/GCE sensing interfaces were investigated for some other representative amino acids, including tyrosine (Tyr), phenylalanine (Phe) (aromatic), histidine (His) (heteroaromatic), and alanine (Ala) (aliphatic). The DPV curves are presented in Figure S12. The $I_{\mathrm{{L}}}/I_{\mathrm{{D}}}$ ratios of these amino acids are in the range of 1.0−1.3, in marked contrast to the ratio of 53 for Trp. The results suggest the high efficiency and specificity of the $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe-based electrochemical sensor for enantiorecognition of Trp.
Quantification of $L$ -Trp in a Racemic Mixture and Urine. Besides discrimination of individual enantiomers, quantitative determination of the enantiomer ratio in a racemic mixture is of great practical significance for many applications. The highly enantioselective recognition for L-Trp makes CuTBPBe@NF/GCE a nice candidate sensor for the analysis of Trp mixtures. As depicted in Figure 7, the DPV peak current response of the electrode to Trp mixtures displays a good linear dependence on L-Trp concentration in the full range of $0{-}100\%$ . Therefore, the Cu-TBPBe@NF/GCE sensing interface can actualize a rapid and quantitative assay of the racemic mixture of Trp isomers.
The application potential of the sensor was further explored for the detection of L-Trp in human urine samples. DPV measurements with Cu-TBPBe@NF/GCE were performed for a series of urine specimens spiked with different concentrations of L-Trp, and the concentrations were calculated based on the calibration curves in Figure 6b. The recovery rates were calculated to be $97-107\%$ , with the relative standard deviations (RSDs) ranging from 2.8 to $3.6\%$ (Table 1). The results are similar to those obtained using the conventional HPLC-UV method. The studies suggest the good reliability and practicability of the Cu-TBPBe@NF/GCE sensing interface for the assay of L-Trp in complex biological systems.
The interference experiments were performed with ascorbic acid (AA), glucose (Glu), and uric acid (UA). As shown in Figures S13 and S14, the presence of these substances even in
10-fold excess ( $\mathbf{\dot{\Omega}}_{1\mathrm{mM}}$ interferent versus $0.1\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{Trp}$ ) does not significantly influence the DPV peak current intensity of $\mathrm{~L~}$ -Trp $(\bar{I_{\mathrm{L-Trp}}})$ or the recognition ratio for Trp enantiomers $\left(I_{\mathrm{L-Trp}}/\right.$ $I_{\mathrm{{D-Trp}}}\rangle$ , suggesting that these substances do not interfere in the quantification of L-Trp.
Recognition Mechanism and Comparison between Cu-TBPBe and $\mathsf{c u}$ -TBPBa. The outstanding enantioselective recognition by Cu-TBPBe could suggest that the framework has a higher affinity toward L-Trp than D-Trp to facilitate more efficient absorption and accumulation of L-Trp on the electrode surface. The higher affinity was further testified by the CD spectra. The powder sample of $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe was dispersed by sonication in the racemic aqueous solution of DLTrp, and the supernatant solution obtained by centrifugation was subjected to CD spectroscopy, for which the supernatant obtained from the $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe dispersion without DL-Trp was used as the background.
Since the MOF is nonporous and keeps its crystalline structure after Trp treatment (Figure S8), the chiral recognition and bias adsorption should take place on the surface. Generally, the adsorption of hydrophilic amino acids on the surfaces should cause the enhancement of the wettability of the material. Thus, the wettability measurements were conducted with glass slides coated with $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe (Figure S15). The original water contact angle on the surface is $64.1^{\circ}$ . After being immersed in L-Trp and D-Trp aqueous solutions, the water contact angles decreased. The water contact angle on the $\mathrm{~L~}$ -Trp-treated surface $(30.4^{\circ})$ is obviously smaller than that on the D-Trp-treated surface $\left(40.0^{\circ}\right)$ . This also manifests the higher affinity of the $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe framework toward $\mathrm{~L~}$ -Trp.
The enantioselective recognition of the MOF is definitely associated with its homochiral $\scriptstyle\mathrm{TBPro}^{2-}$ linkers derived from Lproline. However, the electrode modified with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TBPro}$ free ligand produces very weak and indiscriminate signals for LTrp and D-Trp (Figure S5). This implies that the assembly of the chiral linker and metal ions is critical for the electrocatalytic activity and chiral discrimination. It can be assumed that the hybrid framework provides a specific chiral surface microenvironment in favor of L-Trp adsorption.
To extend the results, we performed similar electrochemical studies with Cu-TBPBa. Cu-TBPBa also shows electrocatalytic activity for the electrochemical redox of Trp, giving obvious CV peaks. However, Cu-TBPBa is incapable of chiral discrimination between L-Trp and D-Trp: the two enantiomers show almost identical CV and DPV profiles at $\mathbf{Cu-TBPBa}(\theta)$ NF/GCE (Figure S16). PXRD measurements confirmed that the MOF maintains the crystalline structure in the PBS media (Figure S17). Notably, Cu-TBPBe and Cu-TBPBa show isomorphous frameworks consisting of the same metal ion, the same chiral ligand, and very similar achiral ligands. The only chemical difference between the achiral ligands (bpee and bpea) lies in the ethene or ethane tether between pyridyl groups. It is rather surprising that the minor difference in the achiral ligand causes so great difference in the capability of chiral discrimination. The discriminative chiral microenvironment is subtly determined, in an almost elusory way, not only by the chiral ligand but also by the achiral coligand. To gain some clues, we compared the local structures of the two MOFs. In $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe, the bpee ligand with the $\mathbf{sp}^{2}~\mathbf{C}{=}\mathrm{C}$ tether adopts a flat quasi-coplanar conformation with the largest deviation of atoms of 0.19 Å. Differently, the $\mathsf{s p}^{3}\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-C}$ tether for bpea in $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBa produces a stepped conformation. The dihedral angle between pyridyl rings is larger for bpea $(22.24^{\circ})$ than for bpee $(12.63^{\circ})$ . The flat structure of bpee is obviously favorable for its interaction with the indole unit of Trp through $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking. Additionally, the chiral proline groups in the proximity of bpee and bpea show different spatial arrangements. The shortest proline−proline distances (represented by the distance between chiral C centers) are $\hat{10.03\:\mathring{\mathrm{A}}}$ in $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe and $9.56\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBa. The spatial arrangement should affect the interactions of Trp with the proline units through hydrogen bonding (Figure 9).
UV−Vis optical spectroscopy can provide further clues for the interactions between the receptor and the substrate.65−68 As depicted in Figure 10, the $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe aqueous dispersion shows an absorption profile in the $250{-}345\ \mathrm{nm}$ range with maximum absorbance at $298~\mathrm{nm}$ . The absorption in the whole range is significantly enhanced upon the addition of L-Trp. The enhancement cannot be simply ascribed to the additional absorption by L-Trp because L-Trp shows very weak and narrow absorption around $286~\mathrm{nm}$ . The absorption enhancement indicates the increase in the probability of the vibronic transitions within the MOF, as a result of the interactions of LTrp with the framework. Control tests were conducted with Lalanine and indole, which correspond to the aliphatic amino acid and aromatic moieties of Trp. The addition of indole to $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe also causes a significant enhancement in UV absorption, suggesting that the indole group interacts with some site of Cu-TBPBe. Alanine should be capable of forming hydrogen bonds with the framework, but it has no obvious effect on UV absorption (Figure 10). This implies that the interactions do not affect the vibronic transitions. However, the amino acid group in Trp interacts with the framework to reinforce the absorption enhancement in collaboration with the indole group, as suggested by the greater enhancement effect of Trp compared with indole. Some other aromatic amino acids, L-Phe, L-Tyr, and $\mathrm{~L~}$ -His, do not cause significant enhancement in the UV adsorption (Figure S18), which indicates weak interactions of these amino acids with the framework. Therefore, the strong effect of L-Trp should be owing to the unique combination of the aromatic indole groups and the aliphatic amino acid moiety, which could allow for synergetic $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and hydrogen-bonding interactions with the $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe framework.
The effect of L-Trp on UV absorption of Cu-TBPBa was also tested. As shown in Figure S20, compared with CuTBPBe, $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBa shows much weaker and blue-shifted absorption as a result of the nonconjugative structure of bpea. The adsorption profile recorded after adding L-Trp can be considered to be the adsorption addition of the two compounds. The different optical response between CuTBPBe and $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBa is in good agreement with the structural difference. The flat conjugative bpee ligand allows an efficient $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction with the indole group of Trp, while the $-\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{-CH}_{2}-$ tether of bpea prevents an effective interaction with L-Trp. Combining the above analyses, the chiral recognition of $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe arises from the synergistic actions between chiral and achiral ligands. The achiral bpee site is surrounded by a number of chiral proline moieties from $\scriptstyle\mathrm{TBPro}^{2-}$ ligands, producing a chiral pocket with multiple interaction sites for enantiodiscriminative adsorption of $\mathrm{~L~}$ -Trp. The bpee site plays an important role by forming $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking with the indole moiety of the analyte. The $\bar{\mathrm{TBPro}}^{2-}$ ligand provides the proline-derived amide groups to form hydrogen bonds with the amino and carboxylic groups of L-Trp. It is assumed that the spatial arrangement of the multiple interaction sites should be critical for discrimination of the enantiomers.
The IR spectra were examined to further prove the interactions between Trp and the $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe framework. As illustrated in Figure 11, L-Trp (or D-Trp) shows the $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ stretching vibrations of the indole group at $3400~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and of the primary amine group at $3011~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (broad), and the band at $1\dot{6}60~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ can be assigned to the carboxylate group.69−71
These bands appear without appreciable shifts in the spectrum of the D-Trp-treated $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe (D-Trp $@\mathrm{Cu}$ -TBPBe). By contrast, obvious red shifts were observed for $\operatorname{L-Trp}(\varnothing\operatorname{Cu-}$ TBPBe: the $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ absorption is shifted to 3388 and 2967 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , and the carboxylate absorption is shifted to $1625~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ These results identify stronger hydrogen bonds between the $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBe framework and L-Trp. The interactions between the $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBa framework and Trp isomers were also studied by the IR spectra. The spectra of L-Trp@Cu-TBPBa and DTrp $@\mathrm{Cu}$ -TBPBa are almost indistinguishable and show the characteristic bands of Trp without significant shifts (Figure S21). The results are consistent with the incapability of the $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -TBPBa framework in enantiorecognition of Trp.
## CONCLUSIONS
Two isomorphous homochiral MOFs were synthesized from an L-proline-derived chiral dicarboxylate ligand and slightly different dipyridyl achiral coligands and used for electrochemical chiral discrimination. Cu-TBPBe led to an efficient sensing interface with high electrocatalytic activity and excellent enantioselectivity for L-Trp, the recognition current ratio being as high as 53. Sensitive quantification of L-Trp in racemic mixtures and urine was demonstrated, providing an intriguing candidate sensor for practical use. By contrast, the isostructural MOF with a similar achiral coligand shows no chiral discrimination for Trp. According to structural and spectral analyses, the enantiodiscriminative capability of CuTBPBe arises from the cooperation between chiral and achiral ligands, which generate a chiral pocket with multiple synergetic sites for biased adsorption of L-Trp.
While previous work with chiral MOF has focused on the effects of chiral ligands, this work gives a new inspiration that a small change in the achiral coligand without changing the overall chiral frameworks can have dramatic or even decisive effects on enantiorecognition performance. This can open new perspectives for the design of new MOFs and other materials for chiral recognition.
